    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/gamal-abdel-nasser"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gamal-abdel-nasser.jpg" alt="Gamal Abdel Nasser" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/gamal-abdel-nasser">Gamal Abdel Nasser</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stevan_Kragujevic,_Gamal_Abdel_Naser_u_Beogradu,_1962_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Stevan Kragujević, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>South Yemen emerged from the end of British rule in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> had held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aden">Aden</a> since 1839 and built it into a major port and military base, while the sultanates, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirates</a> and sheikhdoms of its hinterland formed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aden-protectorate">Aden Protectorate</a> under British treaties. In the 1960s Britain grouped most of these states with Aden in the Federation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-arabia">South Arabia</a>, ruled by conservative sultans, but nationalist opposition grew among Aden’s workers and the tribes of the interior. On 14 October 1963 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-liberation-front-south-yemen">National Liberation Front</a> (NLF) launched an armed uprising in the Radfan mountains, beginning the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aden-emergency">Aden Emergency</a>, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> and urban war against British forces and the federal government. The NLF also fought a rival nationalist group, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/front-for-the-liberation-of-occupied-south-yemen">Front for the Liberation of Occupied South Yemen</a> (FLOSY), backed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gamal-abdel-nasser">Nasser</a>’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, and by 1967 it had defeated FLOSY and taken over most of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> states as their rulers fled. Britain negotiated its withdrawal with the NLF, and on 30 November 1967, as the last British troops left, the People’s Republic of South <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a> was proclaimed in Aden with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qahtan-al-shaabi">Qahtan al-Shaabi</a> as president. The new state abolished the sultanates and sheikhdoms, uniting for the first time under one government a territory stretching from Aden and Lahej to Hadhramaut, Mahra and the island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/socotra">Socotra</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/aden"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aden.jpg" alt="Aden" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/aden">Aden</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MallaDistrict.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Ahmedxalkatheri, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>South Yemen was a poor country of under two million people at independence, most of them farmers, herders and fishermen, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aden">Aden</a> as its only large city. Its economy suffered an immediate shock: the closure of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suez-canal">Suez Canal</a> after the Arab–Israeli war of June 1967 and the departure of the British base removed much of the shipping and spending on which Aden had lived, and the canal did not reopen until 1975. The government pursued a socialist transformation. Agrarian reform laws, above all that of 1970, broke up the estates of sultans and landlords and organised peasants into cooperatives and state farms; fishing was organised in cooperatives; and banks, insurance companies, trading houses and later the Aden oil refinery were nationalised. Remittances from South Yemenis working in Saudi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> and the Gulf states, together with aid from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> and its allies, kept the economy afloat. The regime expanded schooling and literacy, built clinics, and promoted the rights of women: the Family Law of 1974 restricted polygamy, set a minimum age of marriage and gave women rights in divorce, making South <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a>’s family legislation among the most progressive in the Arab world. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> remained the religion of almost the entire population, but the state curbed the power of religious institutions and tribal leaders. Oil found in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shabwa">Shabwa</a> in the 1980s promised revenue only shortly before unification.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/ogaden-war"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ogaden-war.jpg" alt="Ogaden War" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/ogaden-war">Ogaden War</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cubans_in_Ogaden1.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The armed forces of South Yemen were built from the army of the Federation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-arabia">South Arabia</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> fighters of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-liberation-front-south-yemen">NLF</a>, and the ruling party worked hard to bring them under political control. From the early 1970s the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> supplied weapons, training and advisers, and Cuban and East German specialists helped with the army, militia and security services. In return the Soviet navy gained the use of facilities at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aden">Aden</a>, which became an important point of Soviet presence in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horn-of-africa">Horn of Africa</a>. South <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a> supported revolutionary movements in neighbouring states. It provided bases and supplies to the guerrillas of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dhofar">Dhofar</a> rebellion against the Sultanate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oman">Oman</a> until the rebellion was defeated in the mid-1970s, and it backed the leftist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-democratic-front-north-yemen">National Democratic Front</a> in North Yemen. Relations with the Yemen Arab Republic in the north swung between talks on unity and open war: short border wars were fought in 1972 and in 1979, both ended by Arab mediation with renewed promises of unification. South Yemen also clashed with Saudi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> on their border in 1969, and in 1977–1978 it sent military support to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ogaden-war">Ogaden War</a>. The most destructive fighting, however, was internal, when units loyal to rival party factions fought each other in Aden in January 1986.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/qahtan-al-shaabi"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/qahtan-al-shaabi.jpg" alt="Qahtan al-Shaabi" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/qahtan-al-shaabi">Qahtan al-Shaabi</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Qahtan_Shaabi.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>South Yemen was a one-party state that became the only Marxist–Leninist regime in the Arab world. Its first president, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qahtan-al-shaabi">Qahtan al-Shaabi</a>, was removed in June 1969 in what the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-liberation-front-south-yemen">NLF</a>’s left wing called the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corrective-move">Corrective Move</a>, and power passed to a collective leadership including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salim-rubaya-ali">Salim Rubaya Ali</a> as head of the Presidential Council, the party ideologue <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abdul-fattah-ismail">Abdul Fattah Ismail</a> and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ali-nasir-muhammad">Ali Nasir Muhammad</a> as prime minister. The constitution of 1970 renamed the country the People’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/democratic-republic-of-yemen">Democratic Republic of Yemen</a> and created a Supreme People’s Council as its legislature. In 1978 the ruling front merged with smaller leftist parties to form the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemeni-socialist-party">Yemeni Socialist Party</a> (YSP), organised on the Soviet model with a politburo, central committee and a general secretary who held real power. Leadership struggles were frequent and often violent: Salim Rubaya Ali was overthrown and executed in June 1978, Abdul Fattah Ismail became head of state and was pushed into exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> in 1980, and Ali Nasir Muhammad then held the posts of party leader, head of state and prime minister. The regime replaced the old sultanates with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/governorate">governorates</a>, initially known by numbers rather than names, to weaken regional and tribal loyalties. Security services, mass organisations and a people’s militia extended control over society, but personal and regional networks continued to shape party factions.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/national-liberation-front-south-yemen"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/national-liberation-front-south-yemen.jpg" alt="National Liberation Front (South Yemen)" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/national-liberation-front-south-yemen">National Liberation Front (South Yemen)</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Emblem_of_the_National_Liberation_Front_(South_Yemen).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>For more than two decades South Yemen was a distinctive experiment: the only Arab state to declare itself Marxist–Leninist and to build its institutions on the Soviet model. Its revolutionary origins in the struggle against British colonial rule made the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-liberation-front-south-yemen">NLF</a> a symbol for radical movements across the Arab world, and its support for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dhofar">Dhofar</a> rebels and other leftist groups made it a factor in the politics of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabian-peninsula">Arabian Peninsula</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horn-of-africa">Horn of Africa</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cold-war">Cold War</a>. Conservative neighbours, above all Saudi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oman">Oman</a>, regarded it as a threat, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> valued it as an ally and naval foothold at the mouth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a>. Inside the country the regime’s reforms had lasting effects. It dismantled the power of the sultans and sheikhs, unified a fragmented territory under a single administration, extended education and health services, and gave women legal rights and public roles unusual in the region; many southern women worked as teachers, doctors, judges and party officials. These achievements were offset by repression, economic stagnation and the purges and violence of party politics. After unification, the memory of the separate southern state and resentment of northern dominance fuelled southern regionalism, which grew into a movement calling for autonomy or independence for the south.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/ali-nasir-muhammad"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ali-nasir-muhammad.jpg" alt="Ali Nasir Muhammad" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/ali-nasir-muhammad">Ali Nasir Muhammad</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ali_Nasser_Muhammad_USSR_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The crisis that ended South Yemen began within its ruling party. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ali-nasir-muhammad">Ali Nasir Muhammad</a> pursued a more pragmatic course, improving relations with Saudi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oman">Oman</a> and the West, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abdul-fattah-ismail">Abdul Fattah Ismail</a> returned from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> in 1985 to lead the opposition to him. On 13 January 1986 Ali Nasir’s bodyguards opened fire on members of the politburo at a meeting in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aden">Aden</a>, and the confrontation became a short civil war fought with tanks, artillery and naval guns across the city. Thousands were killed, among them Abdul Fattah Ismail, and Ali Nasir fled to North <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a> with tens of thousands of supporters. The victors installed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haidar-abu-bakr-al-attas">Haidar Abu Bakr al-Attas</a> as president, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ali-salim-al-beidh">Ali Salim al-Beidh</a> as general secretary of the party, but the state was gravely weakened. At the same time the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mikhail-gorbachev">Mikhail Gorbachev</a> cut back its aid, and the collapse of communism in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-europe">Eastern Europe</a> in 1989 removed the regime’s external model and support. Talks on unity with the north gained new urgency, encouraged by plans to develop oil in the border region jointly. In November 1989 the two leaderships agreed a unity constitution, and on 22 May 1990 South Yemen united with the Yemen Arab Republic to form the Republic of Yemen, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ali-abdullah-saleh">Ali Abdullah Saleh</a> as president and al-Beidh as vice-president. A southern attempt at secession in 1994 was defeated in a brief civil war.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>South Yemen was born from the anti-colonial struggle against British rule in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aden">Aden</a>, became the Arab world’s only Marxist–Leninist state and a Soviet ally, transformed society and women’s rights in the south, and after a ruinous party civil war in 1986 united with North <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a> in 1990.</em></p>

